<!-- ══════════════════ SECTION 6: LIMITATIONS & RECS ══════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="data-limitations">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">Section 06</div>
      <h2>Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>
      <p class="section-desc">Transparent accounting of dataset constraints is essential for sound policy use. This section also translates findings into actionable recommendations for analysts and decision-makers.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <div class="limit-grid">
      <div class="limit-item">
        <h4>Unbalanced Panel</h4>
        <p>Not all countries report in every year. Coverage ranges from 84 countries (2023) to 162 (2008). Year-to-year comparisons conflate real changes with changes in the composition of reporting countries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-item">
        <h4>Reporting Lag</h4>
        <p>The low 2023 country count (84) primarily reflects data not yet submitted to UNODC, not an actual drop in participation. Conclusions about the most recent year should be treated as provisional.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-item">
        <h4>No Imputation</h4>
        <p>Missing years for a country are simply absent — rows are not present. Analysts computing averages across countries must be careful: each year's mean or median reflects only reporting countries, which may systematically differ from non-reporters.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-item">
        <h4>UK Caveat</h4>
        <p>UK data refer only to England and Wales. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately. Care should be taken not to conflate these entries or use them as a UK aggregate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-item">
        <h4>Territory Double-Counting</h4>
        <p>Some territories (Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) appear as separate entities. Analysts creating regional aggregates must decide whether to include or exclude territories, and be consistent.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-item">
        <h4>Age Data Gaps</h4>
        <p>Age-disaggregated data is absent for sex='both' and only covers a subset of countries from 2003 onward. Age group findings are representative only of reporting countries.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
    <div class="rec-grid">
      <div class="rec-card priority-1">
        <div class="rec-tag">Priority 1 · Immediate Action</div>
        <h4>Caribbean Crisis Response</h4>
        <p>Establish an urgent multi-lateral homicide reduction initiative for Eastern Caribbean small island states, which have seen rates increase 400–700% over the observation period. Haiti, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Ecuador, and Saint Vincent and the Grenadines require targeted international cooperation and security sector reform.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card priority-1">
        <div class="rec-tag">Priority 1 · Gender Policy</div>
        <h4>Male Youth Violence Prevention</h4>
        <p>The 20–44 male age band is the highest-risk demographic globally. Prevention programs targeting gang recruitment, economic opportunity, and conflict resolution for young men in high-rate countries could have the largest absolute impact on homicide counts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card priority-2">
        <div class="rec-tag">Priority 2 · Data</div>
        <h4>Improve Reporting Coverage</h4>
        <p>Roughly 40% of countries have incomplete time series. UNODC and partner organizations should invest in national capacity building — particularly in sub-Saharan Africa and parts of Asia — to improve data completeness and enable more reliable trend analysis.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card priority-2">
        <div class="rec-tag">Priority 2 · Femicide</div>
        <h4>Strengthen Femicide Monitoring</h4>
        <p>While males are over-represented as victims overall, the female burden (particularly 45–59 age group) likely reflects domestic and intimate partner violence. Dedicated femicide reporting mechanisms should be mandated alongside overall homicide data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card priority-3">
        <div class="rec-tag">Priority 3 · Learning</div>
        <h4>Document Success Cases</h4>
        <p>El Salvador, Colombia, Latvia, Philippines, and Sri Lanka all demonstrate that high homicide rates can be substantially reduced. Structured case studies of these successes should be disseminated to help high-burden countries identify transferable policy levers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card priority-3">
        <div class="rec-tag">Priority 3 · Age Disaggregation</div>
        <h4>Expand Age-Disaggregated Reporting</h4>
        <p>Age-specific data coverage is limited to roughly 27 countries in 2023. Expanding this to all UNODC member states would enable far more precise targeting of interventions by age cohort.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /section -->
